# Written BEFORE any Dockerfile exists, deliberately.
#
# `.env` is present in the working tree and holds live Bright Data and model-provider
# credentials. `COPY . .` in a naive first Dockerfile would bake them into an image
# layer, where `docker history` can read them back out even if a later layer deletes
# the file. Excluding it after the fact does not un-publish a layer that already shipped.
#
# Verify after any Dockerfile change:
#   docker history --no-trunc <image> | grep -i env
#   docker run --rm --entrypoint sh <image> -c 'ls -a /app | grep -i "^\.env$"'   # expect nothing

# ---- secrets -------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# ---- vcs / editor --------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github
.vscode
.idea

# ---- dependencies (reinstalled inside the image from the lockfile) -------
node_modules
npm-debug.log*

# ---- eval + test output --------------------------------------------------
# evals/report/latest.json is generated, can be large, and would invalidate the
# layer cache on every run. The suite regenerates it.
evals/report/*.json
evals/.scratch
coverage
*.log

# ---- docs and planning ---------------------------------------------------
# Not needed at runtime. Excluded to keep the image small and the build context
# fast, not because they are secret.
docs
*.md
!README.md

# ---- local tooling -------------------------------------------------------
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore
